      sources: "Sources: Learn, the Graph API pages for each call; Manage PIM policies in Microsoft Graph PowerShell. Chapter 04, “The calls and their permissions,” “Verified facts.”",
      say: `Reading and writing are different jobs here, with different permissions. Every read uses a read-only permission, and the client refuses anything but a read unless it was built for writing, which only happens when you ask to apply. Rule updates and new eligibility need two write permissions; removing a standing assignment is a third task with its own. Learn's PowerShell guide and the tutorial ask for broader permissions, so the code follows the narrower reference pages instead. And a test makes sure the dry run's app never holds a write permission.`,
      detail: `Every call goes through one client, built for one task in one cloud. The client sends only the calls config/calls-ch04.json declares for the task, follows every page on the cloud's own Graph host, and refuses every method but GET unless it was built with -AllowWrite. The entry script builds it that way only with -Apply. Reads and writes are different tasks, with different permissions. List roleManagementPolicyAssignments, with the policy and its rules: RoleManagementPolicy.Read.Directory; delegated Global Reader, Security Operator, Security Reader, Security Administrator or Privileged Role Administrator; all tasks. List roleEligibilitySchedules: RoleEligibilitySchedule.Read.Directory; as above; all. List roleAssignmentScheduleInstances: RoleAssignmentSchedule.Read.Directory; Global Reader (read); all. Update a policy rule (PATCH): RoleManagementPolicy.ReadWrite.Directory; Privileged Role Administrator; pim-apply. Create a roleEligibilityScheduleRequest (POST): RoleEligibilitySchedule.ReadWrite.Directory; Privileged Role Administrator; pim-apply. Create a roleAssignmentScheduleRequest (POST, adminRemove only): RoleAssignmentSchedule.ReadWrite.Directory; Privileged Role Administrator; pim-convert. Learn's Graph PowerShell guide for the same policies asks for RoleManagement.ReadWrite.Directory, and the tutorial for the same, plus Group.ReadWrite.All. Each is broader than the call needs. The code follows the API reference pages, which name the narrower permission. The removal page lists RoleAssignmentSchedule.Remove.Directory as higher privileged than ReadWrite (research gaps, row 43). A test keeps each task's permissions equal to the union of its calls, so a dry run's reader app never holds a write permission. On scopeType: the Graph PowerShell guide uses DirectoryRole in one step and Directory in the next; the list page says both are accepted for Microsoft Entra roles. The code uses DirectoryRole, as the how-to page does.`,
