      sources: "Sources: examples/privileged-access fixtures (fictional tenant). Chapter 04, “Lab 1: fixtures, no tenant,” steps 4–7.",
      say: `Now read what came out. One role was partly set by hand, so its diff is short and touches only what's wrong; what already matches isn't touched. The findings name the people holding standing access, an automation account with no documented owner, and someone eligible outside their group. Then you try Tier zero too early, and the run blocks, one finding for each pilot role not yet done. Applying the pilot wave stops once to make you confirm the activation gate, then sends everything and reads back zero differences. The whole suite passes.`,
      detail: `Step 4, read the diff and the findings: Import-Csv out/pim-wave1/diff.csv | Where-Object Role -EQ 'Exchange Administrator' | Format-Table RuleId, Property, Tenant, Desired, and the findings without PA-PIM-05. Exchange Administrator was partly set by hand, so its diff is short: ten rows in three rules. The authentication context goes from off to c1; approval goes from not required to required, single-stage, by the PIM-Approvers-T1 group; and the active maximum goes from 180 days to 15. Its activation window and eligibility expiry already match, so they aren't touched. The findings name Carl Ops and Erin Patel, who hold Tier 1 roles standing; Contoso-Mail-Automation, a service principal with Exchange Administrator and no owner on the exceptions list; and Dana Reyes, eligible for Exchange Administrator outside the PRV group. The 9 PA-PIM-05 rows ask you to confirm the activation gate. In plan.json, Carl's removal waits for his group's eligibility, and Erin's for a confirmed activation. Step 5, try wave 2, Tier 0, before wave 1 is done: ./Invoke-PaPimRoleSettings.ps1 @lab -Wave 2 -OutputPath out/pim-wave2 prints "Wave 2 (5 roles): 9 Finding, 5 Warn, 2 Info"; "Plan: 30 rule updates, 4 eligibility requests, 0 standing assignments to convert (0 confirmed)"; "Blocked: fix every Finding before -Apply"; "Dry run: nothing was sent". The nine Findings are PA-PIM-07, one for each Tier 1 role that doesn't match its file yet. The two Info rows are the emergency accounts, kept permanent active. Step 6, apply wave 1 to the fake: the first command, with -Apply, stops before any write: "The plan sets an authentication context: confirm its Conditional Access policy is On (Chapter 03), then pass -ActivationGateChecked". The second, with -Apply -ActivationGateChecked, prints the plan again, then "Applied: 51 requests sent; 0 differences from the role files after reading back". The fake lives only for the run, so the next run starts from the fixture again; the tests apply, convert and back out in one session. Step 7, Invoke-Pester -Path tests. Expected result: 330 tests passing, and each row of the gap table seen with your own eyes. Delete out when you're done. This chapter's tests are the 50 in tests/PimEntraRoles.Tests.ps1; with the other chapters' tests in the example, 330 Pester tests pass under PowerShell 7, with no module, tenant or network.`,
