      say: `Each of these dangers has a guard that runs every time. The two worst are the lockout halves, and both are checked against the live tenant before any write. A context switched on before its policy is on looks protective but enforces nothing, which is why a person has to confirm the gate. Then there are the quieter ones: a script nobody reviewed, one identity that can change everything, settings drifting in the portal, and a back-out done from memory. The pattern holds: rules and saved files, not people remembering.`,
      detail: `Approval switched on before the emergency accounts hold Global Administrator: the documented lockout; guard: PA-PIM-02 reads the tenant and blocks every write. Approval with no approver: the default approvers are the people the program keeps eligible; guard: PA-PIM-01 blocks the file. The context set before its Conditional Access policy is On: activation falls back to MFA only if no policy targets the context; an off or report-only policy enforces nothing; guard: PA-PIM-05, and -Apply needs -ActivationGateChecked. A write nobody saw: the tenant changes from a copied script; guard: dry run by default, -Apply only from a reviewed plan, a read-only client throws on any write. A write identity with read-write on everything: one stolen secret changes every role; guard: separate tasks and apps, the dry run's app holds no write permission, a test pins each task's permissions. A sample copied with another tenant's policy ID: the write fails, or lands on the wrong role; guard: policy IDs come only from the read. Settings set in the portal drift from the files: the control in the tenant isn't the one reviewed; guard: the diff, every run; Chapter 08 can add it to the scheduled run (research gaps, row 88). Eligibility longer than the tier allows: a Tier 2 holder is eligible for a year; guard: New-PaPimEligibilityRequest refuses it, and the role's rule is set before any request. Removing a standing assignment before the person can activate: an administrator is stranded mid-conversion; guard: removal only after the group is eligible and the activation is confirmed with a ticket. Tier 0 changed before the pilot is proven: a mistake hits the control plane first; guard: PA-PIM-07 refuses a wave until earlier waves match. A back-out from memory: the rules come back wrong, or not at all; guard: before.json from the run, applied with -RestoreFrom. Names and IDs in the output: the plan names administrators and groups; the output folder is evidence, so delete lab copies.`,
